{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20260524005955ba6944915a7f47ac","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.1","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still failing. The checkbox is the main blocker. Nothing works for checking it:\n- Direct mouse click on the checkbox: didn't work\n- Click on label text: didn't work\n- React onChange dispatch: didn't work\n\nThe Semi Design checkbox is completely resistant to all my approaches. Let me think about why:\n\n1. The Semi Design checkbox uses a custom implementation where the click handler is on the outer wrapper, not on the input element\n2. The checkbox input is visually hidden and the actual clickable element is `.semi-dy-open-checkbox-inner-display`\n3. Even React's onChange handler might not be the right prop to call\n\nLet me try a completely different approach - let me look at what exact events the checkbox handler listens to. I'll inspect the React event handlers more carefully.\n\nActually, looking at the checkbox info from earlier:\n- The outer wrapper is `.semi-dy-open-checkbox`\n- It has children: `.semi-dy-open-checkbox-inner` (contains the visual checkbox)\n- And `.semi-dy-open-checkbox-children` or `.semi-dy-open-checkbox-addon` (contains the label text)\n\nThe click handler is likely on the outer `.semi-dy-open-checkbox` div. When clicked, it should toggle the checkbox. But my mouse clicks aren't working.\n\nWait - maybe the issue is that the checkbox is inside a scrollable container and the bounding box coordinates are off. Or maybe there's a CSS overlay (like the agreement popup) that's intercepting clicks.\n\nLet me try to:\n1. Check if there's any overlay/modal on the page\n2. Use page.dispatchEvent instead of mouse.click\n3. Or use the CDP protocol directly to dispatch the click\n\nActually, let me try using CDP's Input.dispatchMouseEvent instead of Playwright's mouse.click. Or let me try using page.locator('.semi-dy-open-checkbox').click() which uses Playwright's built-in actionability system.\n\nWait, I just noticed something: in my earlier attempt with fill_form7.cjs, the checkbox DID get checked:\n```\nCheckbox state after check(): true\n```\n\nBut the form still didn't submit. And then in fill_form8.cjs, the checkbox stayed false.\n\nThe difference was that fill_form7 used `checkbox.check({ force: true })` while fill_form8 used `checkbox.check({ force: true })` too. So the same method gave different results...\n\nActually, looking more carefully at fill_form7's output:\n```\nCheckbox state after check(): true\nSubmitting...\nClicked create button\n```\n\nSo in fill_form7, the checkbox was checked but the form still didn't submit (no API call). The issue might not be the checkbox but something else entirely. Maybe the input values aren't being read properly from React's state.\n\nLet me take yet another approach. Let me:\n1. Skip the checkbox issue entirely\n2. Try to submit the form programmatically by finding and calling the form's submit handler\n3. Or intercept the API call pattern\n\nActually, I just realized something. When I used `type()` to fill the inputs in the latest script, the React state should be properly updated because `type()` sends actual keyboard events. But maybe the engine select isn't using the right option.\n\nLooking at the output: \"Unity引擎\" is shown as the selected engine, but I clicked \"普通小游戏引擎\". This might mean the select option click is selecting the wrong option.\n\nAlso, I notice the page text shows the form is empty (no values visible). This is because the body text extraction doesn't show input values.\n\nLet me try one more time with a different approach:\n1. Use type() for all text inputs\n2. For the engine, make sure the right option is selected\n3. For the checkbox, use a completely different approach\n\nActually, I think the fundamental issue is that the form is in a micro-frontend (Garfish) and the React state is managed by the child app. The checkbox click needs to go through the proper event handling chain.\n\nLet me try dispatching a click event directly on the checkbox wrapper element using JavaScript:\n```js\nconst wrapper = document.querySelector('.semi-dy-open-checkbox');\nconst event = new MouseEvent('click', { bubbles: true, cancelable: true });\nwrapper.dispatchEvent(event);\n```\n\nBut this might not work because React uses synthetic events.\n\nOK let me try one more completely different approach: use `page.evaluateHandle` to find the React component instance and call its state setter directly.\n\nActually, I think the most productive approach at this point is to try to directly call the create game API. Let me find the correct API endpoint by intercepting the request when the form is submitted properly.\n\nWait - I have an idea. Let me look at the `useCreateApp` JS bundle that was loaded earlier. It might contain the API endpoint.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f456d29-d84c-44e0-89e7-7ad362d1b1bb","uuid":"aae81fa6-cbda-47e2-a65a-e200329d4835"}
